WEBVTT

00:00.270 --> 00:02.640
-: So now we move on to aliases.

00:02.640 --> 00:07.350
So a git alias is just a shortcut, it's a custom command,

00:07.350 --> 00:09.570
a name that we can come up with

00:09.570 --> 00:12.540
that corresponds to some existing git commands.

00:12.540 --> 00:15.000
So instead of typing git status over and over,

00:15.000 --> 00:19.290
I could type git s or git stats or git st.

00:19.290 --> 00:21.540
I can totally customize it.

00:21.540 --> 00:25.110
I can even combine certain commands or add on a bunch

00:25.110 --> 00:27.450
of options, things that get really annoying,

00:27.450 --> 00:30.450
a bunch of flags, and create a shortcut for that,

00:30.450 --> 00:32.580
where instead of typing maybe a hundred characters,

00:32.580 --> 00:34.920
I might type two or three characters.

00:34.920 --> 00:37.770
So the way that we do this is we work

00:37.770 --> 00:41.220
in the global config file, or we set the alias

00:41.220 --> 00:45.000
from the command line, but there is no git alias command.

00:45.000 --> 00:48.060
Instead, we configure aliases just like we set

00:48.060 --> 00:50.100
any other property.

00:50.100 --> 00:53.100
So, I'll show you a very basic example to begin with.

00:53.100 --> 00:55.110
Why don't we do git status?

00:55.110 --> 00:58.080
So instead of typing, git status like this,

00:58.080 --> 01:00.750
I'm in a git repo right now.

01:00.750 --> 01:02.910
Of course it was not gonna work if we're not in one.

01:02.910 --> 01:05.833
Well, it will work, but we won't see this sort of output.

01:05.833 --> 01:06.666
(laughing)

01:06.666 --> 01:08.880
I don't know why I'm reminding you how git status works.

01:08.880 --> 01:10.860
You know that if you run it and you're not in a repo,

01:10.860 --> 01:12.540
it says not in a repo.

01:12.540 --> 01:15.060
Anyway, if I am tired of typing that,

01:15.060 --> 01:17.670
or I type it all the time which I do,

01:17.670 --> 01:20.343
we could set up git s, maybe, or st.

01:21.810 --> 01:26.670
So if I do it in the config file I need to work under alias.

01:26.670 --> 01:28.680
So alias, square brackets,

01:28.680 --> 01:32.190
and then we just follow this syntax s,

01:32.190 --> 01:34.380
if that's what the alias we want to be called,

01:34.380 --> 01:36.870
if we want it to be s, s =

01:36.870 --> 01:40.830
And then some known git command, like status.

01:40.830 --> 01:43.200
Now we don't need to write git status,

01:43.200 --> 01:45.240
we can just write status.

01:45.240 --> 01:49.143
It assumes the git because we'll still need to run git s.

01:50.220 --> 01:51.783
So if I save this file,

01:52.686 --> 01:55.623
and I come over here and I type git s, it works.

01:56.700 --> 02:00.750
And if I instead change that to statpat,

02:00.750 --> 02:03.780
I don't know why, but if that's what I want,

02:03.780 --> 02:07.074
if I try running git statpat,

02:07.074 --> 02:08.010
(laughs)

02:08.010 --> 02:09.450
there we go.

02:09.450 --> 02:12.630
But if I try something else, like git that,

02:12.630 --> 02:16.110
we don't have that command, but I can fix that.

02:16.110 --> 02:17.373
Not that I really would.

02:20.370 --> 02:21.690
And I run it and now it works.

02:21.690 --> 02:23.460
It corresponds to git status.

02:23.460 --> 02:25.860
It's running git status for us.

02:25.860 --> 02:29.310
So I would probably leave that as s or st.

02:29.310 --> 02:31.060
We could do another one for git log

02:32.340 --> 02:36.210
and it would just look like this, l = log.

02:36.210 --> 02:39.570
So, git l would be the same as git log.

02:39.570 --> 02:40.470
git l

02:40.470 --> 02:41.403
And there we are.

02:43.080 --> 02:44.373
Same as git log.

02:45.390 --> 02:48.630
And that's the first most basic type of alias we can do,

02:48.630 --> 02:50.610
just a shortcut, just save.

02:50.610 --> 02:52.230
I mean they're all about saving characters,

02:52.230 --> 02:53.700
but these are very basic,

02:53.700 --> 02:55.740
and we could fill this out with a bunch more.

02:55.740 --> 02:57.600
I'm gonna show you a second way of setting them

02:57.600 --> 03:00.090
from the command line, which some people prefer.

03:00.090 --> 03:01.530
And then we'll talk about aliases

03:01.530 --> 03:04.050
with arguments and then I'll show you some aliases

03:04.050 --> 03:06.990
that, online people have shared, that I don't, you know

03:06.990 --> 03:10.860
I didn't write, but that I use, because they're useful.

03:10.860 --> 03:12.410
So I'll show you those as well.
